WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.197
The cost and latency of every guard.

2
00:00:02.477 --> 00:00:12.291
Put each guard on a ledger with four columns: what it catches, its measured error rates, the latency it adds to the critical path, and the money it costs per request.

3
00:00:12.571 --> 00:00:18.587
Deterministic checks sit at the top of the ledger: microseconds and free, so run them on everything.

4
00:00:18.867 --> 00:00:33.139
A small-model classifier adds a round trip and a per-call cost; Anthropic’s moderation guide works through an example of a billion short posts a month and shows that model choice changes the bill by a factor of about fifty,

5
00:00:33.419 --> 00:00:40.992
so the classifier is usually your cheapest capable model, chosen by measuring it, not by assuming the cheapest is good enough.

6
00:00:41.272 --> 00:00:48.120
An LLM judge on the output adds a full call and, worse, needs the complete answer, which defeats streaming.

7
00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:57.424
OpenAI’s docs make the same point about their moderation endpoint: with streaming, results arrive only after the full output is generated.

8
00:00:57.704 --> 00:01:00.925
You have four ways to reconcile output checks with streaming.

9
00:01:01.205 --> 00:01:04.661
Buffer the whole answer on high-risk routes and accept the wait.

10
00:01:04.941 --> 00:01:11.619
Check incrementally, running cheap pattern checks on each sentence as it streams and stopping the stream if one fires.

11
00:01:11.899 --> 00:01:22.608
Stream and retract, showing text immediately and replacing it if a later check fails (acceptable for low-stakes chat, not for anything a user might act on immediately).

12
00:01:22.888 --> 00:01:33.832
Or move the expensive check off the critical path: run it asynchronously on all traffic or a sample, as monitoring that feeds alerts and your regression set rather than as a gate.

13
00:01:34.112 --> 00:01:42.539
The general rule: gate on cheap, certain checks; gate on expensive model checks only where the risk is high; monitor with everything else.
